#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#     "httpx>=0.27",
#     "python-dateutil>=2.9",   # RFC 5545 recurrence rules, evaluated here
#     "tzdata>=2024.1",   # a slim host carries no system zone database
#     "capabilities-contract==0.4.1",
# ]
# ///
"""
calendar — agent-first CalDAV CLI for remote calendars.

This tool is designed to be invoked by agents. Run `calendar help` to load the
full usage contract on demand — do not rely on memorised knowledge of its
surface.

This is a THIN, project-agnostic adapter over CalDAV (RFC 4791,
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4791): read calendars, read and search
events, compute busy blocks, and book meetings. Any orchestration — booking
rules, which calendar means what, whose slots may be offered — is a CONSUMER's
operation layer that reads this tool's JSON. The tool knows nothing about who
owns a calendar; that lives in the consuming project.

CONFERENCING BOUNDARY — read this before promising a video link:
  Creating an event over CalDAV attaches NO conferencing. This CLI can CARRY a
  meeting URL and cannot MINT one, so `create` reports "conferencing": null on
  every event it books, whatever URL it carried. Run
  `calendar guide conferencing` for where a room should come from instead.

CONNECTIONS
  Configuration requires an explicit connections registry, even for one
  calendar. Where this project keeps its entries, and the machine-level ones it
  inherits, "Where connections live" below says; a project entry replaces that id
  whole, and ids it does not declare are inherited:
    {
      "default": "<id>",
      "connections": {
        "<id>": {
          "backend":     "caldav",
          "address":     "user@example.com",
          "base_url":    "https://caldav.example.com/dav/",
          "secret_env":  "CALENDAR_<ID>_APP_PASSWORD",
          "timezone":    "Europe/Berlin",
          "meeting_url": "https://example.com/room/standing",
          "calendars":   [],
          "allow_write": false
        }
      }
    }

  backend      Transport. "caldav" is the only value that ships; it is stated
               per connection so another transport is a value, not a rename.
  address      The principal's address — the mailbox the calendar belongs to.
               Also selects the connection: --connection user@example.com.
  username     Optional. The Basic-auth login, when the server authenticates
               on something other than the address. Defaults to `address`.
  base_url     The CalDAV entry point. The principal URL is base_url + the
               URL-encoded address + "/". Never hardcoded: it is the one value
               that changes when a provider moves its endpoint.
  principal_url  Optional. The exact principal collection URL, used verbatim
               when a server does not compose one from base_url + address.
  secret_env   The env key holding the password. Absent, CALENDAR_APP_PASSWORD
               is used. The KEY is named here; the VALUE resolves through the
               cascade — project .env/.env.local -> ~/.config/calendar/
               credentials.env -> process env. A secret never sits in the
               registry and never on argv.
  timezone     Optional IANA zone. See TIME.
  meeting_url  Optional standing room URL, attached by `create` when no --url
               is passed.
  calendars    Scope. Empty means every calendar; non-empty restricts both
               reads and writes to those calendars, by display name or href.
               A verb aimed outside the scope exits 4 (out_of_scope).
  allow_write  The write gate, below.

  `calendar guide endpoints` carries the per-provider wiring: which endpoint to
  configure, what credential it takes, and what to do when one is withdrawn.

  Selection: --connection <id|address>, accepted before or after the
  subcommand. Unflagged, the registry's "default" pointer applies, else the
  sole entry; several entries without a pointer is refused as ambiguous.

  Outside any project, connections and doctor are machine reads: they use the
  machine's own connections read-only, unless a grant switched one off; every
  other verb that needs a connection is refused there.

  WRITES ARE OFF BY DEFAULT: a calendar write leaves the system — it creates
  an event other people see and, where the server schedules, mails a real
  invitation to a real human. create/update/delete exit 4 unless the
  connection carries an explicit "allow_write": true. No flag lifts the gate;
  granting it is a deliberate registry edit, made after asking the user.

TIME
  One internal representation: UTC. Every timestamp this CLI emits is UTC with
  an explicit +00:00 offset, so two readings are always comparable.
  Every timestamp it accepts is ISO 8601. An input carrying an offset (or a
  trailing Z) is taken as given. An input WITHOUT one — "2026-09-01T10:00" or
  a bare "2026-09-01" — resolves in the connection's `timezone`, and is
  refused (exit 6, naive_datetime) when the connection declares none. A zone
  is never inferred from the host. A default window starts at midnight in that
  same zone, and ends the stated number of days after whatever start applies.
  All-day events are the exception: they carry a bare date in `startDate` /
  `endDate` with "allDay": true, and their end is EXCLUSIVE, as iCalendar
  defines it. An all-day boundary needs no zone, so `create --all-day` takes a
  bare date from any connection.
  A stored event whose zone this host cannot resolve — a proprietary TZID, or
  a floating time — is placed in the connection's zone and carries
  "zoneFallback" naming what could not be resolved. That instant is an
  assumption; `freebusy` lists every such event under `unresolved_zones`.

RECURRENCE
  Reads are expanded: `events`, `search` and `freebusy` return one entry per
  occurrence inside the window, so a weekly meeting occupies every one of its
  slots and `freebusy` is safe to book against. Each occurrence carries
  "recurrenceId"; its `uid` is the series' uid, and an occurrence the series
  moved or changed carries that revision rather than the series' detail.
  The expansion happens HERE, not on the server. CalDAV has an expand
  extension and this CLI asks for it, but a server may accept the request and
  answer it unexpanded — which looks exactly like a calendar with no
  recurrence until the missing occupancy books a meeting on top of a real
  meeting. Every reply is therefore expanded locally, so the same window gives
  the same answer whichever server served it.
  Writes are single events only. `create` authors no RRULE, and `update` /
  `delete` address the whole stored object — the series, not one occurrence.
  Updating an object that carries per-occurrence overrides is refused (exit 6,
  recurring_event) rather than silently flattening it.

Commands:
  calendar help                               Print this document.
  calendar connections                        Where every connection's values
                                              resolve from (tier, source,
                                              secrets masked). Local only.
  calendar doctor [--connection <id>]         Prove the credential, the
                                              endpoint and the principal —
                                              every connection, or one.
  calendar calendars                          The calendars in scope: href,
                                              name, color, writable.
  calendar events [--calendar C]              Events in a window, occurrences
        [--from ISO] [--to ISO] [--limit N]   expanded. Default window: today
                                              .. +7d.
  calendar show <uid|href>                    One event in full: description,
        [--scan-from ISO] [--scan-to ISO]     url, organizer, attendees with
                                              their PARTSTAT. See UID LOOKUP.
  calendar search <query> [--calendar C]      Substring match over an expanded
        [--from ISO] [--to ISO]               window, client-side. Default
        [--field title|notes|any] [--limit N] window: today .. +30d.
  calendar freebusy --from ISO --to ISO       Merged busy blocks across the
        [--calendars A,B]                     scope. Cancelled events,
                                              TRANSP:TRANSPARENT events and
                                              invitations this address has
                                              DECLINED are not busy.
  calendar create --calendar C --title T      Book an event (write-gated).
        --from ISO --to ISO [--all-day]       Attendees are invited by the
        [--location L] [--notes N] [--url U]  server where it schedules.
        [--invitee EMAIL ...] [--alarm SPEC]  --url defaults to the
                                              connection's meeting_url.
  calendar update <uid|href> [--title T]      Change an event (write-gated).
        [--from ISO] [--to ISO]               Only the flags you pass change;
        [--all-day | --no-all-day]            SEQUENCE is bumped so attendees
        [--location L] [--notes N] [--url U]  see the revision. Guarded by
        [--add-invitee EMAIL ...]             If-Match: a concurrent change
        [--alarm SPEC ...]                    exits 6 (etag_conflict). Moving
        [--scan-from ISO] [--scan-to ISO]     one boundary keeps the other.
  calendar delete <uid|href>                  Remove an event (write-gated).
        [--scan-from ISO] [--scan-to ISO]

  SPEC for --alarm is relative (-15m, -1h, -1h30m, -1d) or an absolute ISO
  datetime. Pass it as --alarm=-15m: a value opening with a minus reads as
  another flag in the separated form. Repeating the flag sets several; on
  `update` the flag replaces the event's whole alarm set.

UID LOOKUP
  `show`, `update` and `delete` take either an href — the exact address
  `events` reports, and the fast path — or a uid. A uid is resolved by a
  server-side UID query; a server that refuses one is answered with a scan of
  a window, which --scan-from / --scan-to set (default -180d .. +365d). An
  href outside the connection's calendars is refused (exit 4) before any
  request is sent.

  Every event carries both `uid` and `id`, the same value under both names, so
  a consumer reading this tool and a local-calendar sibling can use one key.

I/O contract:
  - Success: JSON on stdout, exit 0.
  - Error:   {"error": {"code", "message", "hint"}} on stderr, non-zero exit.

Contract commands (the same on every capability):
  calendar stub                               One-line awareness text.
  calendar manifest --json                    Machine-readable declaration.
  calendar guide [<topic>]                    Guide topics / one guide body,
                                              fetched live from the docs base.
  calendar ids list|get <l>|set <l> <v>|rm <l>  The project identifiers
          [--note <text>]                     envelope — where discovered
                                              calendar hrefs belong, so a
                                              consumer stops re-discovering
                                              the calendar it books into.
  calendar refs                               Menu of the project's reference
                                              files for this capability.
  calendar inventory [--network]              What this capability holds in
                                              this project (--network adds
                                              remote detail).
  calendar context list|show|edit|put <key>   The versioned project documents
                                              behind `refs`.

Exit codes:
  0  success
  2  auth — an unresolved credential, a 401/403 from the server, or a calendar
     this account may not write
  3  not found (no such event, calendar or collection)
  4  policy refusal — the capability gate, a write verb on a read-only
     connection, or a calendar outside the connection's scope. Ask the user;
     never lift a gate yourself.
  5  server / network — unreachable, timeout, 5xx after retries
  6  input / validation (bad datetime, naive datetime with no connection
     timezone, unknown backend, etag conflict, a URL that serves no CalDAV)
"""

import argparse
